\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Two throats at rest: the sign rule (a,b), the force law (c) and the placement calibration (d,e). (a)~Separation change of three $d=12$ pairs one knob apart: like-signed ($\sigma =+1$, heavy dashed) opens, opposite-signed ($\sigma =-1$, solid) closes, and the narrow like-signed pair ($a=1$, light dashed) opens $2.8\times $ more slowly. Separations are inverse-$\chi $-weighted centroids of the midplane $\chi $ pits. (b)~Pull-to-push ratio against the prediction $(Q{+}1)/(Q{-}1)=3/2$ (gold), where $Q=|F_\phi /F_{\rm  grav}|=(a^2+m^2)/m^2=5$ is the scalar-to-gravity force ratio of Eq.~\eqref  {eq:force}: measured $1.518\pm 0.021$ (scatter over $15$ slices); grey, the gauge-settling window $t<3.5$, excluded. (c)~The force law: how far like-signed pairs released from rest at $d=12/14/16/18$ have pushed themselves apart by $t=11.5$ (dots), against inverse-square in the coordinate separation scaled through $d=12$ (grey, dashed) and $A/(d+\delta )^2$ fitted on $d=12$--$16$ alone (gold), dotted where it predicts the $d=18$ rung (Sec.~\ref {sec:pair:force}). If the push were inverse-square in $d$, every dot would lie on the grey curve; the dots rise above it, and the gold fit absorbs the excess with the offset $\delta $. (d)~Per-mouth areal radius of freshly placed pairs at $t=0$ (circles), exceeding the isolated $R_\star $ (dotted) by an excess $\propto d^{-1.16}$. Gold squares: the head-on scout's mouths at the separation reached; open grey squares (here and in e) lie closer than the closest probe. (e)~The scout against the placement curve, $\delta R/R=R_{\rm  scout}/R_{\rm  place}-1$: the $-0.13\%$ plateau to $t=5$ calibrates the tracker's grid snap, and the squeeze then grows to $-4.0\%$ by $t=13$. In the shaded band the curve is held at its last point, so the magnitude is a lower bound; a boost can only raise a coordinate-sphere reading, so a reading below the curve is a genuine squeeze.}}{9}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pair}{{4}{9}{Two throats at rest: the sign rule (a,b), the force law (c) and the placement calibration (d,e). (a)~Separation change of three $d=\clmPairSeparation $ pairs one knob apart: like-signed ($\sigma =+1$, heavy dashed) opens, opposite-signed ($\sigma =-1$, solid) closes, and the narrow like-signed pair ($a=\clmNarrowPairA $, light dashed) opens $\clmNarrowPairRatio \times $ more slowly. Separations are inverse-$\chi $-weighted centroids of the midplane $\chi $ pits. (b)~Pull-to-push ratio against the prediction $(Q{+}1)/(Q{-}1)=\clmScalarQRatio $ (gold), where $Q=|F_\phi /F_{\rm grav}|=(a^2+m^2)/m^2=\clmScalarQ $ is the scalar-to-gravity force ratio of Eq.~\eqref {eq:force}: measured $\clmSignRatio \pm \clmSignRatioErr $ (scatter over $15$ slices); grey, the gauge-settling window $t<\clmSignSettleWindow $, excluded. (c)~The force law: how far like-signed pairs released from rest at $d=12/14/16/18$ have pushed themselves apart by $t=11.5$ (dots), against inverse-square in the coordinate separation scaled through $d=12$ (grey, dashed) and $A/(d+\delta )^2$ fitted on $d=12$--$16$ alone (gold), dotted where it predicts the $d=18$ rung (Sec.~\ref {sec:pair:force}). If the push were inverse-square in $d$, every dot would lie on the grey curve; the dots rise above it, and the gold fit absorbs the excess with the offset $\delta $. (d)~Per-mouth areal radius of freshly placed pairs at $t=0$ (circles), exceeding the isolated $R_\star $ (dotted) by an excess $\propto d^{-\clmPlacementSlope }$. Gold squares: the head-on scout's mouths at the separation reached; open grey squares (here and in e) lie closer than the closest probe. (e)~The scout against the placement curve, $\delta R/R=R_{\rm scout}/R_{\rm place}-1$: the $\clmScoutPlateau \%$ plateau to $t=\clmScoutPlateauEnd $ calibrates the tracker's grid snap, and the squeeze then grows to $\clmScoutSqueeze \%$ by $t=\clmScoutLastInRange $. In the shaded band the curve is held at its last point, so the magnitude is a lower bound; a boost can only raise a coordinate-sphere reading, so a reading below the curve is a genuine squeeze}{figure.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The head-on merger ($d=8$ from rest, $p=0$, opposite signs) and the black hole it makes. Ink: the level-5 arm (level 5 from $t=0$, no restart, no interior device, clean to $t=100$); grey: the level-3 scout. (a)~Areal radius and (b)~Misner--Sharp mass of the scan's MOTS: filled diamonds, offline fine scans (at formation on the scout, later on the level-$3\to 5$ restart chain); open circles, live scans (the level-5 arm until its scan's aperture closes, then the level-3 down-step), each joined within a run of rows and never across a gap; grey line, the level-3 fill twin, whose scan sees through those gaps. Dashed: the fits through the down-step's rows, a line ($-0.0066$ per unit) in (a) and $M_\infty +b\,e^{-(t-36)/\tau }$ in (b). Rules, the initial data: one isolated throat, $R_\star =3.8895$; $\sqrt  2R_\star $, a sphere with both throats' area; $2M_{\rm  ADM}$, the Schwarzschild radius of the pair's mass ($M_{\rm  ADM}=2$ in (b)). The scan's sphere lies inside the MOTS: while the surface forms it retreats inward, where $M_{\rm  MS}$ reads high, and later $R$ wobbles with the surface's shape, while from $t=36$ $M_{\rm  MS}$ falls at every row. (c)~Separation of the $\chi $ pits. (d)~$\max |K|$: the scout runs away at its wall, the level-5 arm walks through. (e)~Grid maxima of $|\phi |$ (solid) and $|\Pi |$ (dashed): the field that held the throats open is swallowed. Hamiltonian norms: Fig.~\ref {fig:constraints}(e). Gold dotted rule: common-MOTS formation ($t=22$); grey dashed rule: the scout's death ($t=26.91$).}}{10}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:headon_collapse}{{5}{10}{The head-on merger ($d=\clmHeadonSeparation $ from rest, $p=0$, opposite signs) and the black hole it makes. Ink: the level-5 arm (level 5 from $t=0$, no restart, no interior device, clean to $t=\clmHeadonLevelFiveEnd $); grey: the level-3 scout. (a)~Areal radius and (b)~Misner--Sharp mass of the scan's MOTS: filled diamonds, offline fine scans (at formation on the scout, later on the level-$3\to 5$ restart chain); open circles, live scans (the level-5 arm until its scan's aperture closes, then the level-3 down-step), each joined within a run of rows and never across a gap; grey line, the level-3 fill twin, whose scan sees through those gaps. Dashed: the fits through the down-step's rows, a line ($\clmHeadonRadiusSlope $ per unit) in (a) and $M_\infty +b\,e^{-(t-\clmHeadonFitStart )/\tau }$ in (b). Rules, the initial data: one isolated throat, $R_\star =\clmRstar $; $\sqrt 2R_\star $, a sphere with both throats' area; $2M_{\rm ADM}$, the Schwarzschild radius of the pair's mass ($M_{\rm ADM}=\clmHeadonADMMass $ in (b)). The scan's sphere lies inside the MOTS: while the surface forms it retreats inward, where $M_{\rm MS}$ reads high, and later $R$ wobbles with the surface's shape, while from $t=\clmHeadonFitStart $ $M_{\rm MS}$ falls at every row. (c)~Separation of the $\chi $ pits. (d)~$\max |K|$: the scout runs away at its wall, the level-5 arm walks through. (e)~Grid maxima of $|\phi |$ (solid) and $|\Pi |$ (dashed): the field that held the throats open is swallowed. Hamiltonian norms: Fig.~\ref {fig:constraints}(e). Gold dotted rule: common-MOTS formation ($t=\clmHeadonMotsTime $); grey dashed rule: the scout's death ($t=\clmHeadonScoutDeath $)}{figure.5}{}}
