\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Constraint evolution of every run the main-text figures draw, each at the highest refinement level it was run at (named in each panel's key): $L^2$ norms of the Hamiltonian ($\mathcal  {H}$, ink) and momentum ($\mathcal  {M}$, grey dashed) constraints. The norms are box averages on the base grid ($\Delta x=0.5$) and compare only within one box (Sec.~\ref {sec:setup:diagnostics}). (a),(b)~The lone throat of Fig.~\ref {fig:single_throat}: level 4 for the unkicked throat and $\varepsilon =\pm 10^{-2}$ ($+10^{-2}$ stopped by hand at $t=13.5$, dot), level 3 for $\pm 10^{-3}$ and $\pm 0.1$, the only level these were run at (dash = sign, weight = amplitude, grey = no kick, crosses = deaths). The base grid does not resolve the seed's shell: at $t=0$ the norms carry the exact data's discretisation floor, to which a kick adds its own defect in quadrature, $0.090\,|\varepsilon |$ ($2.1$--$2.35\times 10^{-3}$ for $|\varepsilon |\le 10^{-2}$), although on the shell it reaches $0.93\times 16\pi |\rho |$ at $10^{-2}$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:model:seeds}). $\mathcal  {M}$ vanishes at $t=0$ and is fed by that defect within the first steps, in proportion to $\varepsilon $ ($2.3\times 10^{-4}$ at $10^{-2}$, $2.3\times 10^{-5}$ at $10^{-3}$, at $t=0.5$, against $2\times 10^{-7}$ unkicked). At level 3 the $|\varepsilon |\le 10^{-2}$ arms then follow the unkicked norms to $5.4\%$ until the fates part---a bound on the box average, not on the violation near the throat (Sec.~\ref {sec:single:collapse}); $\varepsilon =\pm 0.1$ starts $3$--$6\times $ high and dies at $t=14.1$ ($+0.1$) and $15.2$ ($-0.1$) in a one-step overflow at the compactified origin. (c)~The pure-quadrupole collapse of Fig.~\ref {fig:single_collapse} (level 4; gold, its MOTS from $t=33$). The rise from $t\gtrsim 75$ is a grid mode, neither this collapse nor a reflection off the box wall: it starts on the refinement boundaries, every single-throat arm that runs long enough shows it, collapsing or inflating, and the doubled box does not delay it: the norms leave their floor from $t\approx 77$ ($L=128$) and $82$ ($L=64$). (d)~The inflating throat of Fig.~\ref {fig:single_inflation} (level 5, $L=512$) to $t=218$. (e)~The head-on of Fig.~\ref {fig:headon_collapse}, $\mathcal  {H}$ only: the level-5 arm stays below its value at formation (gold, $t=22$) to $t=100$, while the level-3 scout's norm leaves the frame at its wall ($t=26.91$, grey dashed); dashed, the level-3 down-step. (f)~The production spiral of Fig.~\ref {fig:spiral_collapse}: the level-5 leg to its NaN at $t=60.45$ (cross), and the level-3 leg over their common window, which it reproduces to a median $3.5\%/0.7\%$; rules, the merger transient ($t=41.2$) and $\chi $ on its floor ($t=58.4$). At the NaN both norms sit below their transient peaks: the wall is not a constraint blow-up.}}{21}{figure.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:constraints}{{10}{21}{Constraint evolution of every run the main-text figures draw, each at the highest refinement level it was run at (named in each panel's key): $L^2$ norms of the Hamiltonian ($\mathcal {H}$, ink) and momentum ($\mathcal {M}$, grey dashed) constraints. The norms are box averages on the base grid ($\Delta x=\clmDxBase $) and compare only within one box (Sec.~\ref {sec:setup:diagnostics}). (a),(b)~The lone throat of Fig.~\ref {fig:single_throat}: level 4 for the unkicked throat and $\varepsilon =\pm 10^{-2}$ ($+10^{-2}$ stopped by hand at $t=13.5$, dot), level 3 for $\pm 10^{-3}$ and $\pm 0.1$, the only level these were run at (dash = sign, weight = amplitude, grey = no kick, crosses = deaths). The base grid does not resolve the seed's shell: at $t=0$ the norms carry the exact data's discretisation floor, to which a kick adds its own defect in quadrature, $\clmSeedDefectBox \,|\varepsilon |$ ($\clmHamZeroLo $--$\clmHamZeroHi $ for $|\varepsilon |\le 10^{-2}$), although on the shell it reaches $\clmSeedDefectPeak \times 16\pi |\rho |$ at $10^{-2}$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:model:seeds}). $\mathcal {M}$ vanishes at $t=0$ and is fed by that defect within the first steps, in proportion to $\varepsilon $ ($\clmMomKickTwo $ at $10^{-2}$, $\clmMomKickThree $ at $10^{-3}$, at $t=0.5$, against $\clmMomUnkicked $ unkicked). At level 3 the $|\varepsilon |\le 10^{-2}$ arms then follow the unkicked norms to $\clmKickNormSpread \%$ until the fates part---a bound on the box average, not on the violation near the throat (Sec.~\ref {sec:single:collapse}); $\varepsilon =\pm 0.1$ starts $\clmCeilingHamLo $--$\clmCeilingHamHi \times $ high and dies at $t=\clmCeilingDeathPlus $ ($+0.1$) and $\clmCeilingDeathMinus $ ($-0.1$) in a one-step overflow at the compactified origin. (c)~The pure-quadrupole collapse of Fig.~\ref {fig:single_collapse} (level 4; gold, its MOTS from $t=\clmPureQuadMotsTime $). The rise from $t\gtrsim \clmLateConstraintLo $ is a grid mode, neither this collapse nor a reflection off the box wall: it starts on the refinement boundaries, every single-throat arm that runs long enough shows it, collapsing or inflating, and the doubled box does not delay it: the norms leave their floor from $t\approx \clmConstraintFloorBothBoxes $ ($L=128$) and $\clmConstraintFloorSmallBox $ ($L=64$). (d)~The inflating throat of Fig.~\ref {fig:single_inflation} (level \clmFourLevel , $L=\clmFourBoxL $) to $t=\clmFourWall $. (e)~The head-on of Fig.~\ref {fig:headon_collapse}, $\mathcal {H}$ only: the level-5 arm stays below its value at formation (gold, $t=\clmHeadonMotsTime $) to $t=\clmHeadonLevelFiveEnd $, while the level-3 scout's norm leaves the frame at its wall ($t=\clmHeadonScoutDeath $, grey dashed); dashed, the level-3 down-step. (f)~The production spiral of Fig.~\ref {fig:spiral_collapse}: the level-5 leg to its NaN at $t=\clmSpiralDeath $ (cross), and the level-3 leg over their common window, which it reproduces to a median $\clmSpiralLevelMedianHam \%/\clmSpiralLevelMedianMom \%$; rules, the merger transient ($t=\clmSpiralTransientTime $) and $\chi $ on its floor ($t=\clmSpiralChiFloorTime $). At the NaN both norms sit below their transient peaks: the wall is not a constraint blow-up}{figure.10}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces The remnant horizon of the $\varepsilon =+10^{-2}$ collapse: MOTS areal radius (a) and Misner--Sharp mass (b) on the throat-centred scan. All three arms carry the radial kick $\varepsilon =+10^{-2}$: alone at level 3 and, at level 4, dressed with a quadrupole $\varepsilon _2=0.005$ or $0.05$ (floors marked). The fall, by $40\%$ in radius, is the phantom being swallowed. Past the first floor (shaded, curves faint) the scans follow a constraint-violation layer that has reached the MOTS, not the horizon: the rise there, $9$--$11\%$ in radius and $10$--$12\%$ in mass, is numerical and carries no result, since in spherical symmetry a phantom can only shrink a horizon (Sec.~\ref {sec:single:collapse}). The pure quadrupole, which carries no radial kick, only shrinks (Fig.~\ref {fig:single_collapse}(a)).}}{22}{figure.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:single_regrowth}{{11}{22}{The remnant horizon of the $\varepsilon =+10^{-2}$ collapse: MOTS areal radius (a) and Misner--Sharp mass (b) on the throat-centred scan. All three arms carry the radial kick $\varepsilon =+10^{-2}$: alone at level 3 and, at level 4, dressed with a quadrupole $\varepsilon _2=\clmDressedQuadSmall $ or $\clmDressedQuadLarge $ (floors marked). The fall, by $\clmRegrowthShrinkPct \%$ in radius, is the phantom being swallowed. Past the first floor (shaded, curves faint) the scans follow a constraint-violation layer that has reached the MOTS, not the horizon: the rise there, $\clmRegrowthRadiusLo $--$\clmRegrowthRadiusHi \%$ in radius and $\clmRegrowthMassLo $--$\clmRegrowthMassHi \%$ in mass, is numerical and carries no result, since in spherical symmetry a phantom can only shrink a horizon (Sec.~\ref {sec:single:collapse}). The pure quadrupole, which carries no radial kick, only shrinks (Fig.~\ref {fig:single_collapse}(a))}{figure.11}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Death time of the $p=0.12$ spiral under refinement, gauge and start time; every death is an $h_{11}$ NaN except in the $\partial _t\alpha =-\alpha K$ arm, which fails in $K$. (a)~Refinement from one $t=50$ state of the $L=64$ run, maximum level $\ell $ (finest cell $0.5/2^{\ell }$). Filled circles: no matter device, gain per doubling marked; open circles: core matter damping (levels 4--6 share one configuration); open diamond: level 5 at half the time step. (b)~The same wall under other gauges and start times, one row per gauge. The lapse obeys $\partial _t\alpha =-c\,\alpha ^{p}(K-2\Theta )$ and the shift is damped at rate $\eta $; the standard gauge is $c=2$, $p=1$, $\eta =1$, and each other row changes one of the three (and carries the core matter damping, worth under a unit; panel a). Filled circles: level 3 from $t=0$; open circles: level 5 from $t_0$ on $L=64$; open diamonds: level 5 on $L=128$. Level 3: $t=43.6$, $49.0$, $52.1$, $61.9$. Level 5: standard gauge $59.94$ and $60.45$ on $L=128$ ($t_0=0$, $36$) and $55.60$ on $L=64$ ($t_0=50$); $\eta =4$, $60.04$ and $60.05$ ($t_0=50$, $60$).}}{22}{figure.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:spiral_ladder}{{12}{22}{Death time of the $p=\clmSpiralMomentumProd $ spiral under refinement, gauge and start time; every death is an $h_{11}$ NaN except in the $\partial _t\alpha =-\alpha K$ arm, which fails in $K$. (a)~Refinement from one $t=\clmLadderRestartTime $ state of the $L=64$ run, maximum level $\ell $ (finest cell $0.5/2^{\ell }$). Filled circles: no matter device, gain per doubling marked; open circles: core matter damping (levels 4--6 share one configuration); open diamond: level 5 at half the time step. (b)~The same wall under other gauges and start times, one row per gauge. The lapse obeys $\partial _t\alpha =-c\,\alpha ^{p}(K-2\Theta )$ and the shift is damped at rate $\eta $; the standard gauge is $c=\clmGaugeLapseCoeff $, $p=\clmGaugeLapsePower $, $\eta =\clmShiftEta $, and each other row changes one of the three (and carries the core matter damping, worth under a unit; panel a). Filled circles: level 3 from $t=0$; open circles: level 5 from $t_0$ on $L=64$; open diamonds: level 5 on $L=128$. Level 3: $t=\clmGaugeHalvedDeath $, $\clmGaugeHarmonicDeath $, $\clmGaugeStandardDeath $, $\clmGaugeEtaDeath $. Level 5: standard gauge $\clmSpiralFromZeroDeath $ and $\clmSpiralDeath $ on $L=128$ ($t_0=0$, $\clmSpiralRestartTime $) and $\clmGaugeStandardLevelFiveDeath $ on $L=64$ ($t_0=\clmLadderRestartTime $); $\eta =\clmGaugeEta $, $\clmGaugeEtaLevelFiveDeath $ and $\clmGaugeEtaRestartSixtyDeath $ ($t_0=\clmGaugeEtaRestartTime $, $\clmGaugeEtaRestartSixty $)}{figure.12}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Insensitivity of the spiral's burst to the fill window. (a)~$r\,\mathrm  {Re}\,\Psi _4^{2,2}$ at $R=20$ for the freeze arm (fill $1.40/1.90$, ink) and its twin ($1.25/1.75$, gold dots) from the same $t=57$ checkpoint. (b)~Per-sphere envelope of the difference: at most $0.385\%$ of peak at $R=20$ and $0.013\%$ at $R=44$, with ticks at the fill's causal clock $t=57+(R-1.9)$ on each sphere. The difference reaches $R=20$ on that clock and runs ahead of it farther out at $1.2$--$1.3\,c$ (a gauge-speed front), below $1.5\times 10^{-5}$ of peak. The outcome: moving the frozen region's edge moves the waveform by under half a percent, and the burst, which peaks at $R=20$ $13$ units before the core's light cone arrives, cannot depend on the freeze at all.}}{23}{figure.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:fill_insensitivity}{{13}{23}{Insensitivity of the spiral's burst to the fill window. (a)~$r\,\mathrm {Re}\,\Psi _4^{2,2}$ at $R=\clmGwSpiralRadiusIn $ for the freeze arm (fill $\clmGwFillFullA /\clmGwFillStartA $, ink) and its twin ($\clmGwFillFullB /\clmGwFillStartB $, gold dots) from the same $t=\clmGwFreezeStart $ checkpoint. (b)~Per-sphere envelope of the difference: at most $\clmGwFillTwinInner \%$ of peak at $R=\clmGwSpiralRadiusIn $ and $\clmGwFillTwinOuter \%$ at $R=\clmGwSpiralRadiusOut $, with ticks at the fill's causal clock $t=\clmGwFreezeStart +(R-\clmGwFillSkin )$ on each sphere. The difference reaches $R=\clmGwSpiralRadiusIn $ on that clock and runs ahead of it farther out at $\clmGwFillFrontSpeedLo $--$\clmGwFillFrontSpeedHi \,c$ (a gauge-speed front), below $\clmGwFillFrontMax $ of peak. The outcome: moving the frozen region's edge moves the waveform by under half a percent, and the burst, which peaks at $R=\clmGwSpiralRadiusIn $ $\clmGwFillPeakLead $ units before the core's light cone arrives, cannot depend on the freeze at all}{figure.13}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Merger or fly-by: the momentum scan from $d=12$ with opposite signs, from the head-on release ($p=0$) to the fly-by ($p=0.45$). (a)~Both throats in the orbital plane; (b)~their separation. The tracks are the coordinate positions of the $\chi $ pits, followed through each run. Grey, light to dark: the plunges $p=0$, $0.12$, $0.15$, $0.20$ and $0.25$, each drawn until $\chi $ first reaches its floor or the run ends (dots); gold: the fly-bys $p=0.35$ (dashed) and $0.45$. Every arm is level 3 except the $p=0.45$ fly-by, the level-5 arm of the text; since a level-3 pit sits on a cell of side $1/16$, the tracks are averaged over two time units. The fly-by turns at closest approach, $4.8$ at $t\approx 40$; from $t\approx 43$ its mouths have inflated past the scan window, and the faint stretch is the separation of the $\chi $ pits of two inflating mouths, not of two compact throats.}}{23}{figure.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:orbits}{{14}{23}{Merger or fly-by: the momentum scan from $d=\clmPairSeparation $ with opposite signs, from the head-on release ($p=0$) to the fly-by ($p=\clmSpiralMomentumFlyby $). (a)~Both throats in the orbital plane; (b)~their separation. The tracks are the coordinate positions of the $\chi $ pits, followed through each run. Grey, light to dark: the plunges $p=0$, $\clmSpiralMomentumProd $, $\clmSpiralMomentumFifteen $, $\clmSpiralMomentumTwenty $ and $\clmSpiralMomentumTwentyFive $, each drawn until $\chi $ first reaches its floor or the run ends (dots); gold: the fly-bys $p=\clmSpiralMomentumThirtyFive $ (dashed) and $\clmSpiralMomentumFlyby $. Every arm is level 3 except the $p=\clmSpiralMomentumFlyby $ fly-by, the level-5 arm of the text; since a level-3 pit sits on a cell of side $\clmFinestDx $, the tracks are averaged over two time units. The fly-by turns at closest approach, $\clmFlybyClosestApproach $ at $t\approx \clmFlybyClosestTime $; from $t\approx \clmFlybyScanEdgeTime $ its mouths have inflated past the scan window, and the faint stretch is the separation of the $\chi $ pits of two inflating mouths, not of two compact throats}{figure.14}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Mouth growth in the merging ($p=0.12$) and scattering ($p=0.45$) arms, both from $d=12$ with the same throats; the merger's instrumented re-run carries three refinement levels and the fly-by five, so the shared $\tau $ is a result across two resolutions rather than a one-knob comparison. (a)~Areal radius of one mouth (its twin agrees to $2\times 10^{-4}$): solid while the two scan spheres are disjoint, dotted once they overlap; long dash, the common-centre scan. No trapped surface appears on these star-shaped scans, which end at $t=50$ (merger) and $t=100$ (fly-by). (b)~Separation: $p=0.12$ reaches contact, $p=0.45$ misses at $4.8$. (c)~Growth excess $R/R_0-1$ with exponential fits over $t=8$--$25$ (gold): $\tau =3.70$ (merger) and $4.39$ (fly-by).}}{24}{figure.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mouth_growth}{{15}{24}{Mouth growth in the merging ($p=\clmSpiralMomentumProd $) and scattering ($p=\clmSpiralMomentumFlyby $) arms, both from $d=12$ with the same throats; the merger's instrumented re-run carries three refinement levels and the fly-by five, so the shared $\tau $ is a result across two resolutions rather than a one-knob comparison. (a)~Areal radius of one mouth (its twin agrees to $\clmMouthTwinAgreement $): solid while the two scan spheres are disjoint, dotted once they overlap; long dash, the common-centre scan. No trapped surface appears on these star-shaped scans, which end at $t=\clmMouthScanEnd $ (merger) and $t=\clmFlybyEnd $ (fly-by). (b)~Separation: $p=\clmSpiralMomentumProd $ reaches contact, $p=\clmSpiralMomentumFlyby $ misses at $\clmFlybyClosestApproach $. (c)~Growth excess $R/R_0-1$ with exponential fits over $t=\clmMouthFitStart $--$\clmMouthFitEnd $ (gold): $\tau =\clmMouthTauMerger $ (merger) and $\clmMouthTauFlyby $ (fly-by)}{figure.15}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Linearity of the collapsing throat's wave in its quadrupole seed. (a)~$\mathrm  {Re}\,\Psi _4^{2,0}$ at $R=14$, each arm divided by its seed and named at its end: $\varepsilon _2=0.005$, $0.01$, $0.05$ and the kick-free $0.01$ arm collapse onto one curve through the burst; later the smallest arm carries its floor amplified by $1/\varepsilon _2$ and the kick-free arm runs ahead. (b)~rms amplitude over $t=30$--$50$ against $\varepsilon _2$ at three spheres, with the slope-one line through the $0.01$ arm drawn across the decade $10^{-3}$--$10^{-1}$ as a reference. Open circle: the pure quadrupole, within $2\%$ of the kicked arm. Dashed rule: the floor of the level-4 spherical control (the same kick, no quadrupole) at $R=14$, which the line crosses at $\varepsilon _2\approx 2\times 10^{-3}$. The outcome: the wave is the quadrupole's---linear in $\varepsilon _2$ and unchanged, to $2\%$ in amplitude, when the radial kick is removed. The kick alone radiates only the floor; it moves the wave's phase, not its size.}}{24}{figure.16}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:seed_linearity}{{16}{24}{Linearity of the collapsing throat's wave in its quadrupole seed. (a)~$\mathrm {Re}\,\Psi _4^{2,0}$ at $R=\clmGwSeedFigRadius $, each arm divided by its seed and named at its end: $\varepsilon _2=\clmGwSeedWeak $, $\clmGwSeedKicked $, $\clmGwSeedStrong $ and the kick-free $\clmGwSeedPure $ arm collapse onto one curve through the burst; later the smallest arm carries its floor amplified by $1/\varepsilon _2$ and the kick-free arm runs ahead. (b)~rms amplitude over $t=\clmGwSeedWindowLo $--$\clmGwSeedWindowHi $ against $\varepsilon _2$ at three spheres, with the slope-one line through the $\clmGwSeedKicked $ arm drawn across the decade $10^{-3}$--$10^{-1}$ as a reference. Open circle: the pure quadrupole, within $\clmGwPureVsKicked \%$ of the kicked arm. Dashed rule: the floor of the level-4 spherical control (the same kick, no quadrupole) at $R=\clmGwSeedFigRadius $, which the line crosses at $\varepsilon _2\approx \clmGwSeedFloorCross $. The outcome: the wave is the quadrupole's---linear in $\varepsilon _2$ and unchanged, to $\clmGwPureVsKicked \%$ in amplitude, when the radial kick is removed. The kick alone radiates only the floor; it moves the wave's phase, not its size}{figure.16}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17}{\ignorespaces The scalar channel on the $\Psi _4$ spheres. (a)~Fly-by energy through $R=30$ in units of the gravitational energy crossed by $t=80$: gravitational (ink) and scalar with its physical sign (gold, Sec.~\ref {sec:gw:scalar}). The ratio $|E_\phi |/E_{\rm  GW}$ printed at each cut falls from $3.2$ to $0.8$ as the gravitational burst arrives and this running integral drifts (Sec.~\ref {sec:gw:scalar}); past $t\approx 92$ the mouths' areal-radius reading, an upper bound, passes the sphere. (b)~Multipole content, fly-by: $\ell =1$ exceeds $\ell =0$ and $\ell =2$ by $10^{7}$. (c)~Validity window: $|F_\phi |$ at both spheres, with the times at which the mouths' areal-radius reading (an upper bound) passes each sphere (grey rules); left of the blue rule both spheres lie outside the mouths. The $t=60$ window at $R=14$ gives $3.0$ in place of $2.3$.}}{25}{figure.17}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:scalar_channel}{{17}{25}{The scalar channel on the $\Psi _4$ spheres. (a)~Fly-by energy through $R=\clmGwScalarRadiusOuter $ in units of the gravitational energy crossed by $t=\clmGwScalarNormCut $: gravitational (ink) and scalar with its physical sign (gold, Sec.~\ref {sec:gw:scalar}). The ratio $|E_\phi |/E_{\rm GW}$ printed at each cut falls from $\clmGwScalarRatioFifty $ to $\clmGwScalarRatioEighty $ as the gravitational burst arrives and this running integral drifts (Sec.~\ref {sec:gw:scalar}); past $t\approx \clmGwScalarMouthReach $ the mouths' areal-radius reading, an upper bound, passes the sphere. (b)~Multipole content, fly-by: $\ell =1$ exceeds $\ell =0$ and $\ell =2$ by $10^{\clmGwScalarDipoleGap }$. (c)~Validity window: $|F_\phi |$ at both spheres, with the times at which the mouths' areal-radius reading (an upper bound) passes each sphere (grey rules); left of the blue rule both spheres lie outside the mouths. The $t=\clmGwScalarCut $ window at $R=\clmGwScalarRadiusInner $ gives $\clmGwScalarRatioInner $ in place of $\clmGwScalarRatioSixty $}{figure.17}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18}{\ignorespaces Envelope of the scalar flux $|F_\phi |$, horizoned against horizonless arms: a running maximum over $25$ units (one dipole period), smoothed in log amplitude, since the raw flux changes sign every ${\sim }20$ units. (a)~The head-on (level 5{} from $t=0$) at $R=10/14/18$. The common MOTS closes at $t=21.5$ (dotted); the remaining hair leaves as one $\ell =1$ pulse, cresting later at each sphere, then decays (dashed: fits over $t=30$--$95$, $\tau =19/24/29$). The rise before the rule is the open mouths' static hair, canonically ingoing; only the post-horizon window is integrated. (b)~Each encounter on its outermost sphere (head-on and lone throat $R=18$, fly-by and spiral $R=30$): the fly-by grows to the end of its record; the spiral, with a horizon from $t=55.0$ and too young to shed on a ${\sim }20$ clock, is still climbing at its wall ($t=59.9$, dashed rule), beyond which the frozen-core arm is drawn dashed and carries no claim. Mid-grey: the lone throat twice---the $\varepsilon =+10^{-2}$ level-4{} collapse (solid), behind a MOTS from $t=11$, and the unkicked level-4{} throat (dotted), which inflates, horizonless, and grows in the monopole (counted to $t\approx 80$).}}{25}{figure.18}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:scalar_censorship}{{18}{25}{Envelope of the scalar flux $|F_\phi |$, horizoned against horizonless arms: a running maximum over $\clmGwEnvWindow $ units (one dipole period), smoothed in log amplitude, since the raw flux changes sign every ${\sim }\clmGwFluxSignStretch $ units. (a)~The head-on (level \clmGwCensHeadonLevel {} from $t=\clmGwCensHeadonStart $) at $R=\clmGwHeadonRadiusA /\clmGwHeadonRadiusB /18$. The common MOTS closes at $t=\clmGwCensMotsTime $ (dotted); the remaining hair leaves as one $\ell =1$ pulse, cresting later at each sphere, then decays (dashed: fits over $t=\clmGwCensFitLo $--$\clmGwCensFitHi $, $\tau =\clmGwCensTauInner /\clmGwCensTauMid /29$). The rise before the rule is the open mouths' static hair, canonically ingoing; only the post-horizon window is integrated. (b)~Each encounter on its outermost sphere (head-on and lone throat $R=\clmGwHeadonRadiusC $, fly-by and spiral $R=\clmGwScalarRadiusOuter $): the fly-by grows to the end of its record; the spiral, with a horizon from $t=\clmSpiralFlowBirthTime $ and too young to shed on a ${\sim }\clmGwShedClock $ clock, is still climbing at its wall ($t=\clmSpiralFromZeroDeathShort $, dashed rule), beyond which the frozen-core arm is drawn dashed and carries no claim. Mid-grey: the lone throat twice---the $\varepsilon =\clmGwKickedEps $ level-\clmGwKickedLevel {} collapse (solid), behind a MOTS from $t=\clmGwKickedMotsTime $, and the unkicked level-\clmGwSingleLevel {} throat (dotted), which inflates, horizonless, and grows in the monopole (counted to $t\approx \clmGwSingleCountTime $)}{figure.18}{}}
